analytical.level.body></analytical.level></analytical.level.body></analytical.level><analytical.level date.revised="0" date.updated="0"><front date.updated="0"><outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0"><designator date.updated="0">IV</designator><name date.updated="0">Climate Change Policy Development</name></outline.name.block></front><analytical.level.body date.updated="0"><section.block><section date.revised="0" date.updated="0"><metadata.block metadata.store="yes"><metadata field="classification"><value>ENV.III.1.b Environmental law&mdash;Statutory protection of environment&mdash;Environmental assessment&mdash;Provincial assessment</value></metadata></metadata.block><section.front date.updated="0"><outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0"><label date.updated="0">&sect;</label><designator date.updated="0">8:18</designator><name date.updated="0">Generally</name></outline.name.block></section.front><section.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">The development of Manitoba&apos;s climate change strategy began with the work of the Manitoba Climate Change Task Force established in early 2001 by the Province of Manitoba and supported by the International Institute for Sustainable Development.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">For further discussion see <cite finder="manual" type="secondary"><ref ref.id="I0bbba2005f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" short="y">International Institute for Sustainable Development, &ldquo;Climate Change Impacts in Manitoba&rdquo; (March 2007)</ref></cite>, available at <cite finder="manual" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0bbbc9105f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5">https://www.cecmanitoba.ca/cecm.archive/pubs/commission&percnt;20reports/commissioned-reports-2001-2002-manitoba_climate_change_investing_future.pdf</ref></cite> (accessed May 2022).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote> In September 2001, the Task Force published the <ital>Climate Change Task Force Report</ital>.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">2</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><cite finder="manual" type="secondary"><ref ref.id="I0bbbc9115f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" short="y">Manitoba Climate Change Task Force, Manitoba and Climate Change: Investing In Our Future (September 2001)</ref></cite>, available at <cite finder="manual" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0bbbc9125f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5">http://www.cecmanitoba.ca/doc/commission_reports/Commissioned-Reports-2001-2002-Manitoba_Climate_Change_Investing_Future.pdf</ref></cite> (accessed May 2022).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote> The Report addressed the implications of climate change for Manitobans and contained a number of recommendations, including:</para.text><list list.style="block" date.updated="0"><list.item date.updated="0"><name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0"><designator date.updated="0">&bull;</designator></name.block><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">creating a provincial energy policy focused on the development of renewable and alternative energy options and maximizing the energy sector&apos;s potential as an economic development tool with particular focus on hydro, biofuels and hydrogen;</para.text></para></list.item><list.item date.updated="0"><name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0"><designator date.updated="0">&bull;</designator></name.block><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">reviewing <ital>The Manitoba Hydro Act</ital> to determine how Manitoba Hydro should participate in Manitoba&apos;s energy future;</para.text></para></list.item><list.item date.updated="0"><name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0"><designator date.updated="0">&bull;</designator></name.block><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">developing grants and incentives to encourage energy efficiency to build on Manitoba Hydro&apos;s Power Smart Program;<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">3</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">Power Smart was an energy conservation strategy operated by Manitoba Hydro designed to assist consumers with improving energy efficiency via awareness-raising initiatives.</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote></para.text></para></list.item><list.item date.updated="0"><name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0"><designator date.updated="0">&bull;</designator></name.block><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">defining components to be addressed in the establishment of a continental energy policy with the United States and Mexico;</para.text></para></list.item><list.item date.updated="0"><name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0"><designator date.updated="0">&bull;</designator></name.block><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">conducting research and analysis of the effect of climate change on the Prairies;</para.text></para></list.item><list.item date.updated="0"><name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0"><designator date.updated="0">&bull;</designator></name.block><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">establishing a northern strategy to address critical northern issues such as the availability of winter roads and the shipping season for the Port of Churchill;</para.text></para></list.item><list.item date.updated="0"><name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0"><designator date.updated="0">&bull;</designator></name.block><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">adopting new measures for alterative fuels and funding for transit; and</para.text></para></list.item><list.item date.updated="0"><name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0"><designator date.updated="0">&bull;</designator></name.block><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">initiating public education and outreach.</para.text></para></list.item></list></para><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">The <ital>Climate Change Task Force Report</ital> led to the development of Manitoba&apos;s 2002 policy document, <ital>Kyoto and Beyond&mdash;A Plan of Action to Meet and Exceed Manitoba&apos;s Kyoto Targets</ital><footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">4</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><cite finder="manual" type="secondary"><ref ref.id="I0bbc17305f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" short="y">Manitoba, Province of Manitoba Climate Change Action Plan: Kyoto and Beyond&mdash;A Plan of Action to Meet and Exceed Manitoba&apos;s Kyoto Targets (Government of Manitoba Publications, 2002)</ref></cite>, available at <cite finder="manual" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0bbc17315f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5">https://perma.cc/7QMZ-GNE5</ref></cite> (accessed May 2022).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote> which resulted in <ital>Beyond Kyoto: Manitoba&apos;s Green Future</ital> (&ldquo;<ital>2008 Action Plan</ital>&rdquo;).<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">5</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><cite finder="manual" type="secondary"><ref ref.id="I0bbc3e405f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" short="y">Manitoba Science, Technology, Energy and Mines, Climate and Green Initiatives Branch, Beyond Kyoto: Manitoba&apos;s Green Future&mdash;Next Steps: 2008 Action on Climate Change (2008)</ref></cite>.</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote> Notwithstanding the bold title, Manitoba&apos;s climate change strategy recognized and continues to recognize that Manitoba has a relatively small carbon footprint both as a province and on a per capita basis, especially considering Manitoba&apos;s cold winter and warm summer climate, its number of remote communities and its reasonably large rural population. Manitoba&apos;s climate change policy focused on economic development in the form of potential spin-offs, such as the development of additional hydroelectric generating facilities which would allow for the export of relatively clean power to other jurisdictions.</para.text></para><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">For example, Manitoba Hydro&apos;s former Power Smart Program was previously aimed at reducing demand for electricity by residential, commercial and industrial users of electricity in Manitoba. The original motivation for seeking these reductions in consumption was to increase the availability of Manitoba-generated electric power for export into the northern United States marketplace. With the advent of climate change as an issue, however, the Power Smart Program allowed Manitoba Hydro to export surplus power to displace power that would otherwise have been generated from coal-burning facilities in the United States. Manitoba has exported an average of 7283 GW/h of hydroelectricity to the United States annually, displacing approximately 4.9MT of CO<sub>2</sub>e.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">6</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><cite finder="manual" type="secondary"><ref ref.id="I0bbc65505f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" short="y">Manitoba, Manitoba&apos;s Climate Change and Green Economy Action Plan (December 2015), at p. 8</ref></cite>, available at <cite finder="manual" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0bbc65515f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5">https://perma.cc/M8QX-NFUW</ref></cite> (accessed May 2022).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote></para.text></para><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">In 2016, Manitoba Hydro and SaskPower agreed to a 100 <?WGXPPrightHandPageStart sp=";;35" nep=";;37" hyphenated="n" folio="8-35" backFolio="8-36" backBlank="n" releaseNumber="4" releaseDate="2022;12;30" ?>MW electrical power sales agreement which may potentially reduce Saskatchewan&apos;s annual electricity CO<sub>2</sub> production from electricity generation by 200,000 &ndash; 400,000 tonnes.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">7</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><cite finder="manual" type="secondary"><ref ref.id="I0bbc65525f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" short="y">Manitoba Sustainable Development, A Made-in-Manitoba Climate and Green Plan (2017) at p.11</ref></cite>, available at <cite finder="manual" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0bbc8c605f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5">https://gov.mb.ca/asset_library/en/climatechange/climategreenplandiscussionpaper.pdf</ref></cite> (accessed May 2022).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote> More recently, the Government of Canada and the Province of Manitoba together invested more than &dollar;60 million in the construction of the Birtle transmission line<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">8</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><cite finder="manual" type="secondary"><ref ref.id="I0bbc8c615f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" short="y">Manitoba, &ldquo;Transmission Line to Reduce Greenhouse-Gas Emissions and Create More Reliable Power Supply&rdquo;, News Release (March 2, 2020)</ref></cite>, available at <cite finder="manual" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0bbc8c625f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5">https://news.gov.mb.ca/news/index.html?item&equals;46897&amp;posted&equals;2020-03-02</ref></cite> (accessed May 2022).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote> following an agreement to export an additional 215 MW of power to Saskatchewan.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">9</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><cite finder="manual" type="secondary"><ref ref.id="I0bbcb3705f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" short="y">Manitoba Hydro, &ldquo;New Birtle transmission line starts sending power to Saskatchewan&rdquo; (May 21, 2021)</ref></cite>, available at <cite finder="manual" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0bbcb3715f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5">https://www.hydro.mb.ca/articles/2021/05/new_birtle_transmission_line_starts_sending_power_to_saskatchewan/</ref></cite> (accessed May 2022).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote> The new transmission line could potentially reduce Saskatchewan&apos;s annual emissions by an additional 3 MT.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">10</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><cite finder="manual" type="secondary"><ref ref.id="I0bbcb3725f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" short="y">Manitoba Sustainable Development, A Made-in-Manitoba Climate and Green Plan (2017) at p.11</ref></cite>, available at <cite finder="manual" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0bbcda805f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5">https://gov.mb.ca/asset_library/en/climatechange/climategreenplandiscussionpaper.pdf</ref></cite> (accessed May 2022).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote></para.text></para><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">The <ital>2008 Action Plan</ital> originally estimated that Manitoba could achieve GHG reductions of up to 18&percnt; below 1990 levels by approximately 2010, and reductions of up to 23&percnt; by 2012, surpassing Manitoba&apos;s Kyoto equivalent targets.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">11</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><cite finder="manual" type="secondary"><ref ref.id="I0bbcda815f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" short="y">Manitoba, Manitoba&apos;s Climate Change and Green Economy Action Plan (December 2015), at p. 10</ref></cite>, available at <cite finder="manual" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0bbcda825f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5">https://perma.cc/M8QX-NFUW</ref></cite> (accessed May 2022).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote> Initiatives under early iterations of the <ital>2008 Action Plan</ital> focused on hydro, ethanol and energy efficiency. Aware that the aggressive &ldquo;Beyond Kyoto&rdquo; targets were not likely to be met, the Government of Manitoba updated the <ital>2008 Action Plan</ital>, setting a short-term target consistent with Canada&apos;s Kyoto commitment: to reduce GHG emissions from 20 Mt to approximately 17.5 Mt or 6&percnt; below 1990 levels by 2012.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">12</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><cite finder="manual" type="secondary"><ref ref.id="I0bbd01905f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" short="y">Office of the Auditor General Manitoba, &ldquo;Report to the Legislative Assembly: Performance Audits&rdquo; (December 2010) at Chapter 1</ref></cite>, available at <cite finder="manual" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0bbd01915f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5">https://www.cag/mb/ca/audit-reports/report/report-to-the-legislative-assembly-performance-audits/</ref></cite> (accessed May 2022).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote> The target represented a reduction of approximately 25&percnt; from projected emissions under a business-as-usual scenario. The Government introduced more than 60 initiatives to meet that commitment, including legislatively mandating the 6&percnt; emissions reduction target in <ital>The 2008 Climate Change Act.</ital> Manitoba was the first jurisdiction in Canada to adopt the Kyoto target. By 2012, however, the 6&percnt; emissions reduction target had not been met.</para.text></para><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">From October 2013 to January 2015, the International Institute for Sustainable Development led consultations on climate change and the green economy on behalf of the government of Manitoba. In December 2015, Manitoba released an updated action plan which adopted many of the recommendations that had been made during the consultations.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">13</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><cite finder="manual" type="secondary"><ref ref.id="I0bbd01925f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" short="y">Manitoba, Manitoba&apos;s Climate Change and Green Economy Action Plan (December 2015)</ref></cite> available at <cite finder="manual" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0bbd01915f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5">https://perma.cc/M8QX-NFUW</ref></cite> (accessed May 2022).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote></para.text></para><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">Manitoba&apos;s <ital>Climate Change and Green Economy Action Plan</ital> (&ldquo;<ital>2015 Action Plan</ital>&rdquo;) set ambitious new emissions targets and detailed a number of projects, programs, and legislative changes aimed at meeting those new emissions targets. Under the <ital>2015 Action Plan</ital>, the Government of Manitoba committed to reducing GHG emissions by one third over 2005 levels by 2030, one half over 2005 levels by 2050 and to be carbon neutral by 2080.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">14</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><cite finder="manual" type="secondary"><ref ref.id="I0bbd28a05f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" short="y">Manitoba, Manitoba&apos;s Climate Change and Green Economy Action Plan (December 2015), at pp. 3 and 6</ref></cite>, available at <cite finder="manual" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0bbd28a15f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5">https://perma.cc/M8QX-NFUW</ref></cite> (accessed May 2022).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote> Manitoba&apos;s emissions were approximately 20.7 Mt in 2005 and 21.4 Mt in 2013. To meet the 2030 goal under the 2015 Action Plan would require 7.6 Mt of GHG reductions, &ldquo;while absorbing any new emissions from population and economic growth&rdquo;.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">15</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><cite finder="manual" type="secondary"><ref ref.id="I0bbd28a25f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" short="y">Manitoba, Manitoba&apos;s Climate Change and Green Economy Action Plan (December 2015), at p.6</ref></cite>, available at <cite finder="manual" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0bbd4fb05f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5">https://perma.cc/M8QX-NFUW</ref></cite> (accessed May 2022).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote> Projects, programs, and legislative changes aimed at meeting those new emissions targets, included:</para.text><list list.style="block" date.updated="0"><list.item date.updated="0"><name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0"><designator date.updated="0">&bull;</designator></name.block><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">developing a new demand-side management entity to oversee and develop conservation programs to ensure new legislated targets for electricity and natural gas savings are met;</para.text></para></list.item><list.item date.updated="0"><name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0"><designator date.updated="0">&bull;</designator></name.block><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">undertaking a new five-year &dollar;5 million Climate Action Fund in partnership with First Nations communities and invested across sectors;</para.text></para></list.item><list.item date.updated="0"><name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0"><designator date.updated="0">&bull;</designator></name.block><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">promoting investment opportunities in Manitoba&apos;s clean energy economy;</para.text></para></list.item><list.item date.updated="0"><name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0"><designator date.updated="0">&bull;</designator></name.block><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">emissions reduction initiatives and promoting a green economy across sectors (e.g. transportation, buildings and infrastructure, agriculture and rural development);</para.text></para></list.item><?WGXPPrightHandPageStart sp=";;37" nep=";;39" hyphenated="y" folio="8-37" backFolio="8-38" backBlank="n" releaseNumber="4" releaseDate="2022;12;30" ?><list.item date.updated="0"><name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0"><designator date.updated="0">&bull;</designator></name.block><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">introducing new environmental rights legislation and strengthening <ital>The Sustainable Development Act</ital> and <ital>The Environment Act</ital>;</para.text></para></list.item><list.item date.updated="0"><name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0"><designator date.updated="0">&bull;</designator></name.block><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">building and enhancing resilience; and</para.text></para></list.item><list.item date.updated="0"><name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0"><designator date.updated="0">&bull;</designator></name.block><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">exploring and implementing a cap and trade program and carbon pricing mechanisms.</para.text></para></list.item></list></para><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">On October 25, 2017 Manitoba&apos;s Office of the Auditor General released the report, <ital>Managing Climate Change</ital>, a follow-up audit to an earlier 2010 report.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">16</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><cite finder="manual" type="secondary"><ref ref.id="I0bbd76c05f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" short="y">Office of the Auditor General, Manitoba, Managing Climate Change (a follow-up to our December 2010 audit) (October 2017)</ref></cite>, available at <cite finder="manual" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0bbd76c15f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5">https://www.oag.mb.ca/audit-reports/report/managing-climate-change-a-follow-up-to-our-december-2010-audit/</ref></cite> (accessed May 2022).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote> The audit found that despite past government efforts<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">17</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">For examples of departmental climate change initiatives taken during this time, see Manitoba Sustainable Development Annual Reports: 2014-2015; 2015-2016 and 2016-2017, available at <cite finder="manual" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0bbd9dd05f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5">https://www.manitoba.ca/sd/about/annual_reports/index.html</ref></cite> (accessed May 2022).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote> to reduce GHG emissions to the legislated target (6&percnt; below the 1990 level by 2012), there had been &ldquo;little change in Manitoba greenhouse gas emissions over the past decade&rdquo;.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">18</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><cite finder="manual" type="secondary"><ref ref.id="I0bbd9dd15f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" short="y">Office of the Auditor General, Manitoba, Managing Climate Change (a follow-up to our December 2010 audit) (October 2017), at pp. 3 and 8</ref></cite>, available at <cite finder="manual" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0bbd9dd25f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5">https://www.oag.mb.ca/audit-reports/report/managing-climate-change-a-follow-up-to-our-december-2010-audit/</ref></cite> (accessed May 2022).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote> The Auditor General described <ital>inter alia</ital> how gaps and weaknesses in management processes resulted in inadequate plans for updating and setting targets, monitoring and reporting on progress, assessing risks, and adaptation. Recommendations included: reviewing and updating GHG emissions reduction plan at least every three years; using scientific and economic analyses to help set targets; annual reporting on progress and any significant risks to Manitoba as a result of climate change; and developing a clearly defined adaption plan.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">19</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><cite finder="manual" type="secondary"><ref ref.id="I0bbd9dd35f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" short="y">Office of the Auditor General, Manitoba, Managing Climate Change (a follow-up to our December 2010 audit) (October 2017), at p. 29</ref></cite>, available at <cite finder="manual" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0bbdc4e05f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5">https://www.oag.mb.ca/audit-reports/report/managing-climate-change-a-follow-up-to-our-december-2010-audit/</ref></cite> (accessed May 2022).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote></para.text></para><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">Following the Auditor General&apos;s report, the Manitoba Government announced that it had already commenced development of a new <ital>Made-in-Manitoba Climate and Green Plan</ital> (&ldquo;<ital>Made-in-Manitoba Plan</ital>&rdquo;).<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">20</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><cite finder="manual" type="secondary"><ref ref.id="I0bbdc4e15f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" short="y">Manitoba Sustainable Development, A Made-in-Manitoba Climate and Green Plan (2017)</ref></cite>, available at <cite finder="manual" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0bbdc4e25f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5">https://www.gov.mb.ca/asset_library/en/climatechange/climategreenplandiscussionpaper.pdf</ref></cite> (accessed May 2022).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote> The strategic approach, as described in the government&apos;s consultation document, &ldquo;is to lead and innovate in sustainable development&rdquo; supported by the four &ldquo;pillars&rdquo; of climate, jobs, water and nature, under which are 16 &ldquo;keystones&rdquo; for priority action.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">21</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><cite finder="manual" type="secondary"><ref ref.id="I0bbdebf05f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" short="y">Manitoba Sustainable Development, A Made-in-Manitoba Climate and Green Plan (2017), at p. 3</ref></cite>, available at <cite finder="manual" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0bbdebf15f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5">https://www.gov.mb.ca/asset_library/en/climatechange/climategreenplandiscussionpaper.pdf</ref></cite> (accessed May 2022).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote></para.text></para><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">Since the change in government in 2016 from NDP to Conservative, the Manitoba government expressed unequivocal opposition to the imposition of a federal carbon tax. Manitoba has taken the position that it should be given specific consideration by reason that over 98&percnt; of energy being consumed in Manitoba is generated through hydro-electric power and its early efforts in climate change reductions efforts including significant monetary investment in building its clean energy system.</para.text></para><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">In October 2017, as part of developing a Made-in-Manitoba approach, the Manitoba government commissioned and obtained a legal opinion on the authority of Canada to enact carbon tax legislation that would impose a federal carbon tax if Manitoba failed to impose its own provincial tax in compliance with federal targets&mdash;the &ldquo;backstop&rdquo; feature. The opinion concluded that the federal government had authority to impose a carbon tax pursuant to its taxation power under s. 91(3) of the Constitution. Additionally, the scheme&apos;s &ldquo;backstop&rdquo; feature was consistent with the concept of &ldquo;cooperative federalism&rdquo;.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">22</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><cite finder="manual" type="secondary"><ref ref.id="I0bbdebf25f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" short="y">Bryan P. Schwartz, &ldquo;Legal Opinion on the Constitutionality of the Federal Carbon Pricing Benchmark &amp; Backstop Proposals&rdquo; (October 6. 2017)</ref></cite>, available at <cite finder="manual" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0bbdebf35f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5">https://manitoba.ca/asset_library/en/climatechange/federal_carbon_pricing_benchmark_backstop_proposals.pdf</ref></cite> (accessed May 2022).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote> Manitoba, within its Made-in-Manitoba Plan, was proposing a flat carbon price of &dollar;25 per tonne on carbon for the years 2018-2022&mdash;in contrast to Federal government&apos;s standard (at that time) of a carbon levy of &dollar;10 per tonne in 2018, rising by &dollar;10 per tonne each year to &dollar;50 per tonne in 2022.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">23</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><cite finder="manual" type="secondary"><ref ref.id="I0bbe13005f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" short="y">Manitoba Sustainable Development, A Made-in-Manitoba Climate and Green Plan (2017), at pp. 14-19</ref></cite>, available at <cite finder="manual" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0bbe13015f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5">https://www.gov.mb.ca/asset_library/en/climatechange/climategreenplandiscussionpaper.pdf</ref></cite> (accessed May 2022). As of April 1, 2019, the federal fuel charge rates reflect a carbon pollution price of &dollar;20 per tonne of carbon dioxide equivalent (CO<sub>2</sub>e) in 2019, rising by &dollar;10 per tonne annually to &dollar;50 per tonne in 2022.</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote> According to the Manitoba government, the <ital>Made-in-Manitoba</ital> carbon pricing is &ldquo;better for the environment&rdquo; (as it would achieve double the emissions reductions than under the federal plan) and &ldquo;bet<?WGXPPrightHandPageStart sp=";;39" nep=";;41" hyphenated="n" folio="8-39" backFolio="8-40" backBlank="n" releaseNumber="4" releaseDate="2022;12;30" ?>ter for the economy&rdquo; (Manitoba taxpayers would pay approximately &dollar;260 million less than under the federal plan).<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">24</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><cite finder="manual" type="secondary"><ref ref.id="I0bbe3a105f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" short="y">Manitoba Sustainable Development, A Made-in-Manitoba Climate and Green Plan (2017), at pp. 16-17</ref></cite>, available at <cite finder="manual" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0bbe3a115f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5">https://www.gov.mb.ca/asset_library/en/climatechange/climategreenplandiscussionpaper.pdf</ref></cite> (accessed May 2022).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote></para.text></para><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">In February 2018, Manitoba joined the <ital>Pan-Canadian Framework on Clean Growth and Climate Change</ital>, a pan-Canadian cooperative approach to addressing carbon pricing, other climate change measures and clean economic growth. The Manitoba government submitted its <ital>Made-in-Manitoba</ital> proposed pricing plan to the Federal government for its approval. However, the Federal government rejected the plan and imposed its carbon tax scheme on Manitoba. The Manitoba government subsequently took steps to challenge the federal carbon tax in court.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">25</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">See <cite finder="manual" type="secondary"><ref ref.id="I0bbe61205f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" short="y">Manitoba, &ldquo;Canada Welcomes Manitoba to the Pan-Canadian Plan for Clean Growth and Climate Action&rdquo;, News Release (February 23, 2018)</ref></cite>. See also <cite finder="manual" type="secondary"><ref ref.id="I0bbe61215f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" short="y">&ldquo;Manitoba to Challenge Ottawa&apos;s Carbon Tax in Court&rdquo;, News Release (April 3, 2019)</ref></cite>.</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote></para.text></para><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">By October 2021, Manitoba had lost its challenge against the federal government. According to the Federal Court, Manitoba&apos;s assertions of greater emissions reductions were inaccurate and Manitoba&apos;s plan was &ldquo;76,000 tonnes of CO<sub>2</sub> &lsqb;equivalent&rsqb; <ital>less</ital> effective in 2022&rdquo; than a price in accordance with the federal benchmark.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">26</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><cite finder="manual" type="case"><ref p1="Manitoba" p2="Canada (Attorney General)" post.rptr="4640" pre.rptr="2021" ref.id="I0bbe61225f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" title="Manitoba v. Canada (Attorney General)">Manitoba v. Canada (Attorney General), 2021 CarswellNat 4640, 2021 CarswellNat 4641, 2021 FC 1115, 44 C.E.L.R. (4th) 273, 2021 D.T.C. 5118 (F.C.)</ref></cite> at para 52.</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote> Manitoba did not pursue an appeal of the decision.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">27</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><cite finder="manual" type="secondary"><ref ref.id="I0bbe88305f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" short="y">Manitoba, &ldquo;Manitoba Confirms No Appeal in Carbon Tax Case&rdquo;, News Release (November 17, 2021)</ref></cite>, available at <cite finder="manual" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0bbe88315f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5">https://news.gov.mb.ca/news/index.html?item&equals;52739&amp;posted&equals;2021-11-17</ref></cite> (accessed May 2022).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote></para.text></para><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">Carbon pricing issue aside, the Manitoba government continued with implementation of various aspects of its <ital>Made-in-Manitoba Plan.</ital> This includes introduction of <ital>The Climate and Green Plan Act</ital> (&ldquo;<ital>The 2018 Climate and Green Plan Act</ital>&rdquo;) which went into effect November 8, 2018.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">28</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><ital>The Climate and Green Plan Act</ital>, C.C.S.M., c. C134<ital>.</ital></para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote> <ital>The 2018 Climate and Green Plan Act, inter alia</ital>, repealed both <ital>The 2008 Climate Change Act</ital> and <ital>The Sustainable Development Act</ital>.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">29</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><ital>The Climate and Green Plan Act</ital>, C.C.S.M., c. C134, ss. 13(a) and (b).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote></para.text></para><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">In December 2020 and building on the 2016 <ital>Pan-Canadian Framework,</ital> the Federal government introduced its strengthened climate plan, <ital>A Healthy Environment and a Healthy Economy</ital>. Within this plan, the Federal government described its strategy for &ldquo;Climate Smart Agriculture, which included setting a national emission reduction target of 30&percnt; below 2020 levels from fertilizers,<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">30</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><cite finder="manual" type="secondary"><ref ref.id="I0bbeaf405f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" short="y">Environment and Climate Change Canada, A Healthy Environment and a Healthy Economy (2020), at p. 45</ref></cite>, available at <cite finder="manual" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0bbeaf415f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5">https://www.canada.ca/en/services/environment/weather/climatechange/climate-plan/climate-plan-overview/healthy-environment-healthy-economy.html</ref></cite> (accessed May 2022).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote> a matter of particular interest to Manitoba. And as previously discussed in <cite finder="manual" type="secondaryxref"><ref ref.id="I0bbeaf425f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" secref="8:3">&sect;8:3</ref></cite> of this chapter, with the passing of the June 2021 <ital>Canadian Net-Zero Emissions Accountability Act</ital>, Canada enshrined in legislation, <ital>inter alia</ital>, a national emissions reduction target of 40-45&percnt; below 2005 levels by 2030 and commitment to achieve net-zero greenhouse gas emissions in Canada by 2050.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">31</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><ital>Canadian Net-Zero Emissions Accountability Act</ital>, S.C. 2021, c. 22, at ss. 1.1(2), 4 and 6.</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote></para.text></para></section.body></section></section.block></